2026-06-30 15:12:15,064 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e19fe9da-5b6a-4883-8a80-37deec53626f', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nACE_0342-02_JB02.raw\nACE_0475_JB02.raw\nACE_0504_JB02.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\ndentification of Cross-linking partners of BpA modified p37 and p97  by photo cross-linking MS\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nwe wanted to see where and how the protein p37 interacts with its partner proteins p97, PPI,Sds22 and I3. To this end p37 derivatives with genetically encoded crosslinking amino acid (p-benzoyl-L-phenylalanine) were generated. After incubation with the partner proteins,  photocross-linking and tryptic digest the resulting cross-linked peptides were analysed by mass spectrometry.\n\n[METHODS]\nSample preparation for LC/MS/MS A volume corresponding to 30 µg of protein from each cross-linking sample was Methanol/Chloroform precipitated (Wessel & Flügge, 1984). The protein pellet was rehydrated in 8M Urea and 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABC) and the proteins subjected to in-solution digestion (ISD). Briefly, Proteins were reduced with DTT (5 mM) for 30 min at room temperature. Protein reduction was followed by alkylation with iodoacetamide (IAM, 20 mM, 30 min, room temperature) and quenching of excess IAM with DTT (final concentration DTT 30 mM). Samples were digested with LysC (1 µg) for 3h at 37°C. After adjusting the urea concentration to 0.8M urea with 50 mM ABC the samples were digested for 16h with trypsin (1 µg) at 37°C. The digestion was stopped by adding formic acid (FA) to a final concentration of 5%. The tryptic digests were then desalted on home-made C18 StageTips as described (Rappsilber et al., 2007). Briefly, peptides were immobilized and washed on a 2 disc StageTip. After elution from the StageTips, samples were dried using a vacuum concentrator (Eppendorf) and the peptides were taken up in 0.1% formic acid solution (10 μL) and directly used for LC-MS/MS experiments (see below for details).  LC-MS/MS settings MS Experiments were performed on an Orbitrap LUMOS instrument (Thermo) coupled to an EASY-nLC 1200 ultra-performance liquid chromatography (UPLC) system (Thermo). The UPLC was operated in the one-column mode. The analytical column was a fused silica capillary (75 µm × 46 cm) with an integrated PicoFrit emitter (New Objective) packed in-house with Reprosil-Pur 120 C18-AQ 1.9 µm resin (Dr. Maisch). The analytical column was encased by a column oven (Sonation PRSO-V2) and attached to a nanospray flex ion source (Thermo). The column oven temperature was set to 50 °C during sample loading and data acquisition. The LC was equipped with two mobile phases: solvent A (0.1% formic acid, FA, in water) and solvent B (0.1% FA in 80% acetonitrile, ACN). All solvents were of UPLC grade (Sigma-Aldrich). Peptides were directly loaded onto the analytical column with a maximum flow rate that would not exceed the set pressure limit of 980 bar (usually around 0.5 – 0.8 µL/min). Peptides were subsequently separated on the analytical column by running a 240 min gradient of solvent A and solvent B (start with 9% B; gradient 9% to 60% B for 200 min; gradient 60% to 100% B for 20 min and 100% B for 20 min) at a flow rate of 300 nl/min. The mass spectrometer was controlled by the Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tune Application (version 3.3.2782.28) and operated using the Xcalibur software (version 4.3.73.11). The mass spectrometer was set in the positive ion mode. Precursor ion scanning was performed in the Orbitrap analyzer (OTMS; Fourier Transform Mass Spectrometry) in the scan range of m/z 370-1400 and at a resolution of 120000 with the internal lock mass option turned on (lock mass was 445.120025 m/z, polysiloxane) (Olsen et al. 2005). Product ion spectra were recorded in a data dependent fashion in the OTMS in a variable scan range (“auto”) and at resolution 30000.  The ionization potential (spray voltage) was set to 2.3 kV. Source fragmentation was switched on (energy “10V”). Peptides were analyzed using a “Top Speed” regime (repeating cycle of full precursor ion scan (AGC target “standard” or 50 ms) followed by dependent MS2 scans for 5 seconds (minimum intensity threshold 5×104)). The precursor ions were isolated using the quadrupole (isolation window 1.4 m/z). After Higher-energy C-trap dissociation (HCD) (normalized collision mode set to “stepped” and normalized collision energy set to “23, 28, 35%”) the product ions were measured in the OTMS (AGC target “50000” or 150 ms, mass range “normal”). During MS2 data acquisition dynamic ion exclusion was set to 30 seconds. Only charge states between 3-8 were considered for fragmentation.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
